# NeruYomi — Technical Memo

## Overview

NeruYomi is a single-window desktop application built with Tauri v2. The frontend is a single HTML page (`index.html`) that loads seven JavaScript modules in sequence. There is no bundler, no framework, and no virtual DOM — the UI is constructed directly from vanilla JS with DOM APIs. The Rust backend handles all filesystem operations, native dialogs, network proxying, and the update pipeline.

---

## Process Architecture

The application runs as a WebView2 window (Windows), WKWebView (macOS), or WebKitGTK (Linux) hosted inside a Tauri shell. The JavaScript frontend communicates with the Rust backend exclusively through `invoke()`, a typed async IPC bridge that serialises calls to JSON and dispatches them to `#[tauri::command]` handlers. There is no HTTP server — all communication is over Tauri's internal IPC channel.

A second communication direction exists for Rust-to-JS events: the library file watcher fires a `library-changed` event over Tauri's event system, received in JavaScript via `tauriListen()`. This function reads from `window.__TAURI__.event.listen` first and falls back to the `__TAURI_INTERNALS__` path only if the public API is unavailable.

The asset protocol (`asset://localhost/` on macOS/Linux, `https://asset.localhost/` on Windows) is used to serve local files — cover images and PDF source files — directly to the WebView without IPC or base64 encoding. This is the primary reason reading performance is fast: image URLs are path-transformed strings, not memory transfers.

---

## Module Load Order and Dependencies

Scripts are loaded synchronously in this order:

```
pdf.min.js        — PDF.js worker (third-party)
utils.js          — Pure helpers, Tauri bridge, icon SVGs
store.js          — Data persistence layer, MU API shims
settings.js       — Theme engine, font management, settings panel
mu.js             — MangaUpdates metadata, cover queue, throttle
update.js         — Update checker and installer
library.js        — Library loading, rendering, chapter management, reader state
reader.js         — Page rendering, strip mode, zoom/pan, navigation
index.html        — State objects, init sequence, view helpers
```

Each module declares its dependencies in a comment header. There are no ES modules or imports — everything shares a single global scope. This is intentional: it avoids a build step and keeps the development loop as fast as saving a file and reloading.

