## MangaUpdates Integration (`mu.js`)

`fetchMuInfo(name, force)` is the single entry point. It checks `muCache` first unless `force` is true. If not cached, it:

1. Checks `muOverrides` for a title substitution
2. Calls `muSearch()` (proxied through the Rust backend in Tauri) with up to 5 results
3. Selects the best match by exact title equality, falling back to the first result
4. Calls `muFetchSeries()` for the full record, since the search endpoint omits `authors` and `pub_status`
5. Parses the response into a normalised info object: `{ id, title, url, description, image, thumb, year, status, genres, authors, rating, cachedAt }`
6. Writes the result to `muCache` and calls `saveMuCache()`

Both `muSearch()` and `muFetchSeries()` are wrapped in `_muThrottle()`, a promise-chain serialiser that ensures no two MU API calls are fired within 500ms of each other regardless of call origin. The chain resets to a fresh `Promise.resolve()` when the queue empties, tracked by a `_muQueueSize` counter, so the chain does not grow unbounded over a session.

In browser dev mode, both functions hit a local proxy server on port 3005 rather than the Tauri backend. This proxy exists because the MangaUpdates API does not send CORS headers that permit direct browser requests.

`applyMuInfoToPanel(info, series, updateCover)` writes fetched metadata to the series hero panel DOM: status inline in the stat row alongside year and rating, author, description, genre tags, and the MangaUpdates link.

