## Theme Engine

The theme engine (`settings.js`) derives all surface colours algorithmically from three inputs: `ink` (base dark colour), `accent`, and `paper` (base light colour). `deriveTheme()` computes eight surface steps by shifting lightness in HSL space, then writes all values as CSS custom properties on `:root` via `_applyVars()`. The depth, steps, and contrast parameters control how aggressively the surfaces are separated.

Six built-in themes are defined as CSS class overrides (`body.theme-arctic`, etc.) that re-declare the same custom property set. Custom themes use inline styles on `:root` directly and are stored in `custom_themes.json`. Font overrides are stored separately in `theme_fonts.json` and applied per-theme on load.

---

## Update System (`update.js`)

On launch, `getAppVersion()` resolves the running version from the Tauri backend. `checkForUpdates()` calls `invoke('check_for_updates', { currentVersion })` which performs a GitHub releases API query in Rust and returns a structured result: `{ hasUpdate, currentVersion, latestVersion, releaseUrl, downloadUrl, downloadFilename }`.

If an update is available, `showUpdateBanner()` renders a notification bar. The "UPDATE NOW" button only appears when `downloadUrl` is non-empty — i.e. when the Rust backend found a platform-specific installer in the release assets. A "Download" link pointing to `RELEASES_URL` is always rendered as a fallback.

`autoUpdate()` calls `invoke('download_and_install_update', ...)` and listens to `update-download-progress` Tauri events to drive a progress bar. On success it prompts the user to close the app to complete installation. On failure it shows the error inline alongside a retry button and the fallback download link.

