## Navigation and View Management

The application has four named views: `landing`, `library`, `chapters`, and `reader`. Only one is visible at a time. `show(id)` swaps the active `.view` element by toggling the `active` class, animating in the new view unless `ui.navBack` is set. `bc(view)` handles everything else that changes on navigation: the breadcrumb trail in the header, the nav-row path segments, the enabled state of back/forward/rescan buttons, and the right-side action area. It also sets `document.body.dataset.view` to the current view name so CSS can target view-specific overrides without JavaScript.

`mobileBack()` pushes the current view onto `ui.navHistory` and navigates up one level. `mobileForward()` pops from `ui.navHistory` and navigates forward. The stack is cleared on any forward navigation to prevent stale forward destinations.

The header breadcrumb strips leading zeros and reformats chapter names by parsing the volume and chapter numbers through `parseChapterKey()`, so a folder named `Vol.02 Ch.0006.5 - Some Title` is displayed as `Vol.2 Ch.6.5 Some Title`.

Select mode is automatically exited when navigating away from the library view — `show()` checks `ui.selectMode` and clears all selection state if the destination is not `library`.

