## Reading Progress

Progress is stored in `readProgress`, a nested object keyed by `seriesKey` (`local:Series Name`) at the top level and `chapterName` at the second level. Each chapter entry holds: `page`, `total`, `read`, and `updatedAt`.

`setChProg()` writes a chapter's current page and total, computing `read` automatically: a chapter is marked read when the reader reaches the first page in RTL mode (page index 0) or the last page in LTR mode (page index total - 1). Once `read` is set to true it is never unset by normal reading — only by `toggleChRead()`.

`saveCurrentProgress()` is called on every page turn. It writes to the in-memory `readProgress` object synchronously, updates the card progress bar immediately, then debounces the actual disk write to 1000ms. This means storage is written at most once per second regardless of reading speed.

`findContinueIdx()` determines which chapter and page to resume from. It first looks for a chapter that is in-progress (page > 0, not marked read), then falls back to the first unread chapter, then falls back to the last chapter if everything is read.

`seriesReadStats()` counts chapters with `read: true` and returns a count and a ratio for driving the card progress bar and the hero panel progress display.

---

## Bookmarks

Bookmarks are stored as a flat object in `bookmarks.json`, keyed by series key (`local:Series Name`). `isBookmarked(key)` is a direct property lookup. The bookmarks tab is a filter pass over `library.items` — no separate data structure. Toggling a bookmark re-renders the library immediately.

---

## Series Identification and MU Overrides

Each series is matched to MangaUpdates by its folder name. When the automatic match is wrong, the identify modal allows searching MangaUpdates directly and selecting the correct entry.

`applyMuOverride(rec)` stores `{ title, seriesId }` in `muOverrides`, keyed by the local folder name. On the next `fetchMuInfo()` call for that series, the stored title is used as the search query instead of the folder name. After applying an override, the old cached cover file is deleted via `invoke('delete_cached_cover', ...)`, the MU cache entry and cover index entry are cleared, and the series is immediately re-opened to trigger a fresh fetch.

`clearMuOverride()` performs the same cache clearing and re-open without writing a new override entry.

---

## Custom Covers

`invoke('pick_image_file')` opens a native file picker restricted to image types. The selected file is passed to `invoke('cache_custom_cover', { srcPath, seriesName })` which copies it into the app cover cache directory and returns the cached path. The path is written to `coverIndex[series.name]` with `{ cachedPath, custom: true }` — the `custom: true` flag prevents the MU cover queue from overwriting it. Resetting a custom cover deletes the cache entry, re-enqueues the series through `enqueueMuCover`, and updates the card and hero panel.

---

## Rescan

`rescanLibrary()` is context-aware. It always starts with a single `read_dir` call on the library root to get the current folder list (excluding `_trash`). It builds a Map from the existing `library.items` for O(1) lookup, then builds a new array preserving existing series objects by reference wherever the folder name matches. New series have chapters loaded and are pushed to a `newEntries` list for cover loading. The `library.items` array is mutated in-place so references held by other parts of the code remain valid.

After the library diff, behaviour splits on `ui.currentView`:

- **Library view** — `renderLibrary()` is called and a toast shows the diff summary.
- **Chapters or reader view** — the open series' chapter list is re-fetched from disk and the chapter list is re-rendered in place. If the currently open series was removed, the app falls back to the library view. If the view is `chapters`, `fetchMuInfo(name, true)` is additionally called with `force: true` to refresh MangaUpdates metadata for that series.

---

## File Watcher

When a library is opened, `invoke('start_library_watcher', { path })` registers a Rust-side `notify` watcher on the library root. When new directories appear, the Rust backend fires a `library-changed` event. JavaScript receives this via `tauriListen` and sets a 1500ms debounce timer before calling `_loadLibraryFromPath()` again, which prevents multiple rapid events from triggering redundant scans. Toasts fired during the initial 2-second startup window are suppressed via a `_toastReady` flag to prevent false-positive messages on auto-reopen.

---

## Chapter Name Parsing

`parseChapterKey(name)` extracts `[volume, chapter]` as numerics from a folder name. It handles the following formats via regex:

- Volume: `Vol.02`, `Vol 2`, `Volume 02`, `Volume2`
- Chapter: `Ch.0006.2`, `Ch 6.2`, `Chapter 006`, `Chapter6.5`
- Fallback: first numeric run in the name

The separator in decimal chapter numbers (`.` or `_`) is normalised before `parseFloat` so `Ch.0006_2` and `Ch.0006.2` both produce `6.2`. Volumes are parsed but not used for sorting — only chapter number determines order.

`cleanChTitle()` strips the volume/chapter prefix, language tags in parentheses (`(en)`, `(jp)`, etc.), and group names in square brackets from a folder name to produce a human-readable title. `extractGroup()` returns just the group name from the trailing bracket.

---

## Library Sort

`applySortToList(list)` sorts an array of series objects in-place according to `ui.libSort`. Volume count is computed by `getVolumeCount(s)`, which iterates `s.chapters`, parses each chapter name with `parseChapterKey`, collects distinct non-zero volume numbers into a `Set`, and returns the count (falling back to 1 if chapters exist but carry no volume prefix). Size comparisons treat `null` as 0. All non-alphabetical sorts use `natCmp` as a tiebreaker.

The sort is applied inside `renderLibrary()` on a shallow copy of the filtered list so `library.items` order is never mutated. Sort preference is persisted to `settings.json` via `patchSettings` and restored in `_loadLibraryFromPath()` before the first render.

---

## Multi-Select and Trash

Select mode is toggled by `toggleSelectMode()`, which sets `ui.selectMode`, adds `body.select-active` (used by CSS to dim unselected cards), and re-renders the library. While active, card `onclick` handlers call `toggleSeriesSelect(name)` instead of `openSeries()`. `toggleSeriesSelect` updates `ui.selectedSeries` (a `Set`) and toggles the `.selected` class on the affected card directly without a full re-render.

The trash action bar is a `position: sticky; bottom: 0` element inside `#view-library`, visible only when `ui.selectMode` is true.

`moveSelectedToTrash()` iterates `ui.selectedSeries` and calls `invoke('move_to_trash_folder', { seriesPath, libraryPath })` for each. The Rust command canonicalises both paths and verifies the series is inside the library root before calling `std::fs::rename`. On success the series is removed from `library.items` in JavaScript. After all moves, `toggleSelectMode()` is called to exit select mode and re-render.

The `_trash` folder is excluded from `library.items` at every entry point: FSAPI load, Tauri load, and rescan.

